{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon Textract announces accuracy and feature updates to DetectDocumentText and AnalyzeDocument APIs - AWS","context":"Amazon Textract, a machine learning service that extracts text and data from documents and images, has announced updates to its text detection model used in DetectDocumentText and AnalyzeDocument APIs. The new features include support for superscripts, subscripts, and rotated text in documents. Accuracy improvements have been made for text detection in box forms, distinguishing visually similar character sets, and processing lower-resolution documents like faxes. These updates are now available in multiple AWS regions, including US East (Ohio, N. Virginia), US West (N. California, Oregon), Asia Pacific (Mumbai, Seoul, Singapore, Sydney), Canada (Central), Europe (Frankfurt, Ireland, London, Paris, Spain), and AWS GovCloud (US-East, US-West). Users can access the Amazon Textract console to get started and learn more about its capabilities by visiting the Amazon Textract website, developer guide, or resources page","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/06/amazon-textract-detectdocumenttext-analyzedocument-apis/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"querying-aws-cloudwatch","skill_description":"Runs SQL queries on CloudWatch Logs data exported as Apache Iceberg tables in S3 Tables. Covers VPC Flow Logs, WAF logs, CloudFront access logs, Route 53 resolver logs, Network Firewall logs, EKS audit logs, Verified Access logs, SES logs, VPC Lattice logs, Step Functions logs, NLB access logs, S3 Server Access Logs, and 20+ other AWS vended data sources. Applies when analyzing network traffic, investigating security incidents, querying exported logs with SQL, enabling S3 Tables integration, configuring log export, correlating logs with other data, running Athena queries on the aws-cloudwatch table bucket, analyzing S3 access patterns, S3 latency investigation, S3 cost attribution, or bucket access auditing.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, s3tables, athena, glue, s3\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-cloudwatch"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Analyze your CloudTrail events using natural language in Amazon Q Console - AWS","context":"AWS CloudTrail, a service used for recording API activity across AWS accounts for security auditing, compliance, and operational troubleshooting, now integrates with Amazon Q Console. This integration enables users to investigate AWS account activity using natural language. Users can ask Amazon Q Console about CloudTrail configuration, query logged events for security investigations, and troubleshoot operational issues without writing queries or manually parsing log files. With this integration, users can check if CloudTrail trails are properly configured, identify gaps in logging coverage, and confirm which data event sources are being tracked. For security concerns, users can ask about who accessed specific IAM roles, changes made to VPC configurations, and unauthorized access attempts. For operational troubleshooting, users can ask about who created or deleted resources, identify API calls generating errors, trace activity from specific IP addresses, or determine why bills spiked. Amazon Q Console can query CloudTrail trails, associated CloudWatch log groups, and event data stores on behalf of users, providing answers grounded in actual account activity. This integration is available in all AWS commercial regions where Amazon Q Console is supported. To get started, users can open Amazon Q in the AWS Management Console and ask questions about their CloudTrail configuration or account activity. For more information, visit the AWS CloudTrail documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/09/cloudtrail-amazon-q-console/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"querying-aws-redshift","skill_description":"Enables Redshift system-table (SYS_*) log publishing to S3 Tables in Apache Iceberg format for both Provisioned clusters and Serverless namespaces, verifies publishing status, and queries the published logs via any Iceberg-compatible engine including Redshift and Athena. Covers system tables such as sys_query_history, sys_query_text, sys_connection_log, sys_query_detail, and sys_session_history. Applies when turning on S3 Tables log publishing for a cluster or namespace, confirming publishing status and locating the S3 Tables namespace, querying non-realtime data from Redshift system tables off-cluster at scale, or building dashboards for Redshift monitoring and auditing, especially for historical or high-volume system-table data beyond the in-cluster SYS_ view retention window. Trigger phrases: publish redshift system table log to s3 tables, enable-logging s3 tables, describe redshift logging status, query redshift system tables in athena or redshift, redshift log exports to iceberg.\n\nServices: redshift, s3tables, athena, glue\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, audit, configure, monitoring, diagnose, dashboard\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-redshift"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify whether Textract AnalyzeDocument calls are logged as CloudTrail management events", "parent_id": "e32a919c-e318-4843-b9d6-c42aa2d6e4ab"}

